decisions.descriptionMarkdown:
Tests are organized into seven lanes named by where they can run: A behaviour (test platform), B browser, C native offscreen pixels, D native windowed platform, E Android, F iOS, G local real GPU. The scope table is the contract.

- Every gpux test belongs to exactly one lane through its location and selector:
  - `crates/*/tests` and `crates/gpux_conformance/tests/lane_a.rs` for A;
  - `lane_c.rs` for C;
  - the desktop host for D;
  - `tooling/web-e2e` for B;
  - the Android and iOS hosts for E/F;
  - `gpux-testkit lane g` for G.
- Cadence: A, C and D run on every PR on all three desktop OSes. B runs a PR subset plus a nightly full set. E and F run nightly and on mobile/renderer/catalog path changes. G is local only.

Test-support input additions sit beside Zed's input model, never as a parallel layer, and reach the window through the same paths platforms use.

- `VisualTestContext`/`TestAppContext` gain:
  - IME composition through the window's input handler (marked-text replace, update, unmark/commit, cancel);
  - `simulate_key_up`;
  - `simulate_click_count(position, button, count)`;
  - `simulate_drag(path, button, modifiers)`, which emits down, held-button moves and up.
- `TestWindow`/`TestPlatform` replace `unimplemented!()` in minimize, zoom, show-window-menu, hide, reveal-path and run with recorded calls that tests can read.
- A test window options struct sets logical size, scale factor and display size; the defaults stay unchanged.

A new publishable crate, `crates/gpux_conformance` (package `gpux-conformance`, lib `gpux_conformance`), owns the conformance suite.

**Catalog (`scenes/`).** Each `SceneSpec` has:
- id and covered areas;
- logical size and scales;
- lanes;
- a build function returning the root view;
- an interaction `Script`: steps such as Locate, Click, Drag, Wheel, Touch, Type, Ime, Key, Advance, ExpectSemantics, ExpectBounds, Probe, Snapshot;
- expectations.

**Hosts.** A `Host` trait runs scripts:
- `TestHost` on `TestPlatform`, with real text, the deterministic clock and an optional headless renderer;
- `AppHost` inside a real platform app, with in-app input injection, `render_to_image` and result lines.

**Reporting.** A line protocol, `gpux-conformance: PASS|FAIL|SKIP <scene> <detail>`, which `gpux-testkit` parses from stdout, logcat or the browser console.

**Image comparison** (`d-compare`).

**Hosts as examples of the crate:**
- `desktop_host` (bin, lane D);
- `web_host` (cdylib, lane B);
- `android_host` (cdylib with `android_main`, lane E);
- `ios_host` (staticlib, lane F).

Lane A and C suites are integration tests `tests/lane_a.rs` and `tests/lane_c.rs`.

A new publishable crate, `tooling/testkit` (package `gpux-testkit`, bin `gpux-testkit`), is the single entry point.

- **Commands:** `lane <ids…> [--list|--scene|--grep]`, `web build|serve|e2e`, `android build|run`, `ios build|run`.
- **Behaviour:**
  - it orchestrates cargo, wasm-bindgen, pnpm/Playwright, cargo-ndk/Gradle/adb and xcodegen/xcodebuild/simctl;
  - it resolves Windows `.cmd` shims;
  - it checks host prerequisites with actionable errors;
  - it parses `gpux-conformance` result lines;
  - it ends every command with `gpux-testkit: <p> passed, <f> failed, <s> skipped`, exiting nonzero on failure or zero selected tests;
  - it writes artifacts under `artifacts-temp/testkit/<lane>/`.
- **Windows-runner software adapters:** CI passes `GPUX_WGPU_ADAPTER=software` explicitly for lane C on runners without a GPU.

`gpux-testkit lane g` runs only on a developer machine. It runs:

- lane B in the `local-gpu` profile (optionally headed), including `@timing` and `@dpr2`;
- lanes C and D on the hardware adapter, with relative probes only, since hardware goldens are never committed;
- `cargo bench -p gpux-platform --bench randomized_element_tree` and the `#[gpux::bench]` suites, with results summarized under `artifacts-temp/testkit/g/`.

It refuses to run when `CI` is set.

`docs/testing.md` is the human contract for the lanes:

- lane table, commands and prerequisites per host;
- CI cadence;
- golden update flow;
- environment variables (`GPUX_WGPU_ADAPTER`, `GPUX_UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS`, `VK_DRIVER_FILES`);
- the verified Chromium flags and troubleshooting.

The following are rewritten to route validation through gpux lanes, keeping RayX only as a consumer:
- `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` (Validation, Consumers and AgentX sections);
- the gpux skill (`SKILL.md` validation routing, `references/multithreaded-wasm-apps.md`);
- `docs/installation-and-usage.md`.

New knowledge goes into existing domains: lanes and tooling into `repository/validation`, web into `platform/web`, mobile into `platform/mobile`, the renderer into `renderer/wgpu`. The epic's decisions are promoted after completion.
